The government of Egypt accepted Germany's help to use it as support at the site.
"Fortunately there was no time," a spokesman for the Federal Interior Ministry said.
On September 19, the assailants had hijacked a group of travelers composed of 5 Germans, 5 Italians, a Romanian as well as their 8 Egyptian companions.
After the abduction in Egypt's southern desert, the hostages were deported side-to-the-back in the border area between Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad.
The identity of the hijackers remained unknown to the end as well as the details of the release or possible ransom payments.
In the meantime, the saved people came to Cairo.
Steinmeier said the released hostages were under the guardianship of Germany's embassy.
He didn’t say any words about the details of liberation.
At first in Berlin there was no information about their return to Germany.
