Victim accident identified by police
The 63-year-old pedestrian who died at the hospital has been identified after being hit by a vehicle in Essex County.
Carole Paulson of Chelmsford Road in Brentwood was implicated in the crash that occurred on Shenfield Road Friday afternoon.
The woman was taken to Queen's Hospital in Romford but later died.
Following the accident, a 51-year-old man was arrested on suspicion of negligent driving.
Since then he has been released on bail.
Turkey widens its diplomatic horizons
Long before Turkey intended to be a member of the European Union, the European powers nested the desire to penetrate the interior of this country.
In the eastern part of the Bosphorus, perhaps only 20 steps inland Asia is one of the most interesting relics of disastrous imperial ambition.
Hydarpasha train station was designed by two German architects, Otto Ritter and Helmut Conu, in neo-Renaissance style.
At the very least, this is what the guides say.
The building is tipped at the end of the platforms and has in front two towers, one for each corners, looking like a typical German town hall.
The stone's dirty.
The building in your complex is a bit decadent.
But once the bocquery to vault, there is no doubt of the greatness of the enterprise that this was the most western gate.
The station was inaugurated in August 1908 by the German consortium Anatolia-Bagdad Corporation.
Germany, a "latecomer" in the imperial game, had the ambition to dominate the Middle East through this railroad being curated to Damascus, Baghdad, and further into what is now Saudi Arabia.
However, the ambitions of this imperial Germany failed during the First World War.
Its railways were attacked by Lawrence of Arabia and his irregular Arabs.
Due to the relative isolation of the surrounding regions, generated by the frozen borders of the Cold War, it fell low
British dominance and as forces advanced into Egypt, they pushed the Turks and their German allies out of Palestine and Syria.
The news was also bad for Hyderpasha station.
In 1917 a train of ammunition exploded destroying its elaborate roof.
Turkish sources said it was sabotage.
Since then, the station has declined and stayed out of history.
But today, this railway, gateway to Anatolia and all the eastern centers, serves – at least symbolically – to highlight Turkey’s willingness to broaden its diplomatic horizons.
The Justice and Development Party that is with the government - the AKP - has planned a new foreign policy for the country.
The relative isolation of the surrounding regions, generated by the frozen borders of the Cold War, has disappeared.
Now there's a new policy of collaboration.
The Turkish government excellently maintains good relations with Israel and Syria, with both the Palestinian factions of Hamas and Fatah, with Iraq and Iran and, of course, with the European Union and the United States.
In order to assess the parameters of this new foreign policy, I climbed into a bunk and went to the Turkish capital Ankara, in order to visit one of the AKP leaders, an expert in foreign policy, Turkish Prime Minister Suat Kiniklioglu.
The next morning - about ten and a half hours later - after a jolting trip, which produced the cadences of the ancient world in the famous documentary The Night Mail, I arrived in the Turkish capital - new city, designed to govern a new secular state, away from the Ottoman past.
However, the AKP, with its clear Islamist roots, represents a paradigmatic change in Turkish consensus
that has raised some suspicions in the West and especially in Washington.
A taxi takes me to the headquarters of the AKP, an ultra-modern structure of stone and steel – built ad hoc – in a developing suburb, surrounded by half-built shopping malls and “power pastries” where middle-class managers in business suit – men and women — meet early in the morning to agree/do business.
Suat Kiniklioglu could be a centre-right politician of almost any party in Europe.
Suit, tie, I'm sure he's still got a Blackberry.
"The objective of the party's foreign policy is to re-integrate Turkey into its immediate neighbourhood.
The Cold War was an anomaly," he tells me.
Turkey has been cut into relations with its interior.
Today you have to look at the same time towards East and West.
It’s not a case if it’s not for one or the other.
He's got to do both.
"But surely at some point," I asked, "Turkey might be forced to choose?"
"Turkey," he replies, "does not want to face that choice."
He then underlines a point that almost everyone I have spoken to up to that point had recited me almost by heart: Turkey's relations not only with the Middle East, but with the Caucasus region, and with the entire Black Sea, represent a benefit for Europe and the United States as for Turkey itself.
The message seems to be: with Turkey on board, Europe in its complex can metaphorically mount those railway lines that open the world towards East Asia and the Middle East.
Again at Hydarpasha station, after a second sleepless night, my thought returns to the two German lords, Herr Ritter and Herr Conu, who had attended the construction of this building 100 years ago.
How surprised to learn that Europe’s ambitions in the Middle East, in large part, would today be in the hands of Turkey?
And waiting for the terminal the ferry that will take me back through the Bosphorus, I remember that in those days Turkey was considered "the sick man of Europe."
So, there are not many doubts in what will be the continent to which this fascinating country must claim.
Vernon Handley and William Woodruff
Mark Brown writes: which Guildford musical director since 1962, Vernon "Tod" Handley (September 11) was responsible for the Guildford Philharmonic Orchestra and the Choir.
As part of the musical project for the city, in the following year he founded his magnificent youth choir, Proteus.
It was supposed to be for those under 21, but by the time the Proteus sang its end in 1979, several of us were running out of age 20 - let's only refuse to hit the choir.
The name "Proteus" (the mythological being changer of forms) represented the will of the choir and the ability to perform any music that is presented to it, whether madrigal or Messiaen, indifferently unaccompanied or with full orchestra.
The musical versatility produced by this young choir was often surprising, always exciting, and it was all due to that man’s inspiration and the enthusiasm for music that remained absolutely constant over the years.
Tod often told us to "let the music speak for itself."
He’d say, “The composer knows what he wants.
It's all there in music.
That’s how you just have to sing!”
His wand was always absolutely clear and precise, although never imposed, or too flowery.
Whatever the musical scatter, always ended at an even greater level.
John Frayn Turner writes:
If any driver should've been a gentleman, this is Vernon Handley.
I knew him very well during the two decades in which he directed the Guildford Philharmonic from 1962 to 1982.
Especially in the last 40 years, I’ve reviewed more than 100 of their concerts and I can honestly say that I’ve never, by anyone, been disappointed – otherwise I’ve often been inspired by them.
Tod Handley had that rare combination of greatness and extreme personal modesty.
He hated drivers who, in his words, simply “sold one or two well-knowned warhorses all over the world.”
We'll miss a lot.
William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
In his sequela by William Woodruff (September 25) you state that "in 1946 the studies of economic history were resumed at Oxford and in 1950 he won a Houblon-Norman research grant... at the same time he was studying for a bachelor's degree, a master's degree and a doctoral one."
This is learned in a note from Woodruff’s author in Beyond Nab End, but it’s not totally correct.
From 1946 to 1953 he was professor of economic history at the University of Nottingham, during which he completed a doctoral thesis on the British rubber industry, supervised by Professor J.D. Chambers, and later published in 1958 with the title The British Rubber Industry.
He spent the years between 1950 and 51 in the United States, enjoying a Houblon-Norman scholarship, and the summer of 1952 at the universities of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
In 1953 he definitively left Nottingham for Illinois.
The reason Woodruff opted to overlook this part of his career, remains unknown.
