Second round for Honda's bestsalers
In the month of November Honda will begin the commercialization of the second generation of Jazz.
If its predecessor already had a wide habitability, this new minivan has an even wider interior space.
To all this are added refined mechanics, which increase driving comfort and save more fuel.
Evolving in the face of revolution.
That's the second generation of Honda Jazz.
Althus aesthetically the new Jazz presents a line very similar to the model that happens, the changes introduced in this minivan are numerous.
Among others, it is worth mentioning an innovative design, a new platform, larger dimensions and new mechanics.
However, before analyzing the new model it must be said that this new Jazz comes six years after introducing the first generation on the market.
Therefore, a first observation is that Honda has renewed this model in a shorter term than that used by other manufacturers.
Although in Europe, Jazz is less well-known, in other markets this model is a sales phenomenon.
This is the case of Japan, a country where Jazz has been the most marketed car in 2002 and also during 2008.
Precisely in Japan about a million Jazz units have been sold since its launch.
The second market has been that of the Old Continent, with about half a million units in six years.
However, Honda intends that this new Jazz will increase sales within the segment.
Thus, while in 2007 the Japanese firm traded a total of 83,000 units of the Jazz, in 2009 the estimates point to 100,000 cars, which would mean a growth of 17% in the European market.
Regarding the Spanish market, Honda has marketed a total of 8,000 Jazz units since 2002, which represents 6.7% of Honda’s total sales in Spain.
Regarding the new generation, those in charge of the Spanish subsidiary expect to sell 2,000 units of the Jazz in a full year.
Precisely the arrival of the new Jazz will allow Honda to greatly alleviate the decrease in sales in Spain.
In fact, those responsible for the brand expect to end the year with a total of 22,000 units marketed, which is 4,000 less than in 2007, a decrease of 16%.
Despite this increase in Honda sales has increased its market share in Spain, reaching 1.8%, compared to 1.6% last year.
In addition, the Japanese firm expects to achieve 2% in 2010.
To achieve this quota Honda is confident in the arrival of another new model.
It is about the Insight, a hybrid of five doors and compact size - 4.37 meters in length - of which the Japanese company expects to commercialize about 200,000 units a year worldwide.
By markets and according to estimates from the Japanese manufacturer, 50% of the Insight’s sales will occur in the United States, while the remaining percentage will be distributed between the Old Continent and Japan.
The commercial launch of the Insight is planned for the first months of the year, once its presentation is made in society in January in Valencia.
Regarding the first generation, the new Jazz grows in all its heights.
Its length amounts to 3.90 meters (five centimeters more), the width up to 1.69 meters (two centimeters more) and the wheelbase to 2.50 meters, which means an increase of five centimeters.
The height, on the other hand, does not vary and remains at 1.52 meters.
Together with the increase in dimensions, the interior habitability also grows.
This gain is due, on the one hand to the inclusion of a new platform and, on the other, to the introduction of the fuel tank in the central position, a solution also adopted in the previous Jazz.
Also, the capacity of the trunk - which has a double-bottomed system - rises to 399 liters, 19 liters more capacity of the trunk of the first generation.
It should be noted that these measurements are of the version with the 1.4 engine, since the trunk capacity of the Honda Jazz 1.2 is 379 liters.
Other improvements - in addition to exterior elements such as rear LEDs or the possibility of mounting the panoramic glass - is an improvement in access to the rear seats.
And it is that the doors that give access to the second row have an opening angle of 80 degrees, compared to the 67 degrees of the first Jazz.
They are also new engines in this second generation of Jazz.
At the beginning of its commercialization, this small model will have two gasoline thrusters of 1.2 and 1.4 liters.
Both mechanics incorporate i-VTEC technology that achieves lower consumption, emits less carbon dioxide, achieves excellent mechanical refinement and at the same time achieves a high torque throughout the engine speed.
The first 1.2-liter mechanique obtains power from 90 horsepower to 6,000 revolutions with a maximum torque of 114 Nm to 4,900 laps.
This thruster - which in previous Jazz reached 78 horses - has an expenditure of 5.3 liters and emissions of 125 grams of CO2.
Regarding the previous thruster, which in the previous generation achieved 83 horses, it now happens to have 100.
This Jazz 1.4 of 100 horses achieves a combined consumption of 5.4 liters and 128 grams of CO2.
'Bee car' that avoids collisions
To advance the development of technologies for the prevention of accidents Nissan has been inspired, this time, in nature, in particular, in bees.
The Japanese company has created a robot that, based on the flight of bees, avoids accidents.
This is the Bio-mimetic Car Robot Drive, or BR23C that is the first step in collision prevention technologies.
Engineers at the Nissan Motor Co. Advanced Technical Center have developed this small robot in collaboration with the Center for Advanced Science and Technology Research at the prestigious University of Tokyo in Japan.
Kazuhiro Doi, Managing Director of Public Relations for Technology and Brand Management Research and Development, the BR23C is presented as an expansion of Nissan’s Four-Areas Safety Shield.
This robot intends to reinforce the collision prevention capacity of the brand’s vehicles.
During their flight, each bee creates its own oval-shaped personal space that closely resembles the Nissan Safety Shield.
But the most important thing is the bee’s compound eyes, able to see with an angle of more than 300 degrees in all directions, allowing it to fly uninterrupted within its own personal space.
To recreate this visual function, the engineers designed what they’ve called a Laser Range Finder.
It detects obstacles that lie up to two meters away within a radius of 180 degrees in front of the BR23C, calculates the distance to said object and sends a signal to an on-board microprocessor which immediately results in collision prevention.
"The moment it detects an obstacle, the small robot will mimice the movements of a bee and immediately change direction by turning its wheels at right or greater angles to avoid the collision.
The biggest difference with any existing system is that the evasion maneuver is totally instinctive.
If not, the robot would not be able to react quickly enough to avoid obstacles," explains Toshiyuki Andoh, director of the Mobility Laboratory and chief engineer of this project.
Unliking to a bee, the robot cannot deflect upwards or downwards; it can only move in two dimensions and in the direction that allows it to rotate its wheels and not in infinite directions and ways like bees.
For this reason, Nissan has used a rotation function, in addition to acceleration and deceleration, so that the small robot avoids collisions.
"This device only needs to process information every few seconds, and act according to that data.
It doesn’t require saving information or intuitive logic from previous manoeuvres, so it can work continuously with just a couple of dozen KB of memory.
This may surprise, but we’re not talking about giggles of memory, not even megas.”
This is the first step in developing new instant collision prevention technologies, a technology that Nissan predicts that will be introduced into standard cars within ten years.
The robot uses lithium-polymer batteries, which are lighter and last longer than the variety of lithium ions.
Another possible future application of this technology could be the integration of the unit with a GPS satellite navigation system to guide the driver to their destination avoiding obstacles along the way.
